# Caddyfile — Debian target.
#
# Certificates come from Let's Encrypt over DNS-01, against the Cloudflare zone.
# Three names are issued: the bare PUBLIC_DOMAIN plus jellyfin. and seerr.
#
# NOT HTTP-01, which is what this file used to do. The box is VPN-only
# (decisions.md D33): the router forwards the WireGuard port and nothing else,
# so an HTTP-01 challenge has no path in. DNS-01 needs no inbound path — Caddy
# writes a TXT record in the zone and Let's Encrypt reads it. The cost is a
# provider module, and therefore caddy/Dockerfile.
#
# Requirements on the target:
#
#   - A records for PUBLIC_DOMAIN, jellyfin.PUBLIC_DOMAIN and
#     seerr.PUBLIC_DOMAIN pointing at the box's LAN address, DNS-only (grey
#     cloud). A private address in public DNS is useless to the internet and
#     resolves correctly for both LAN and tunnel clients.
#   - CF_API_TOKEN in .env, scoped Zone:DNS:Edit on this zone alone.
#   - ACME_EMAIL set — Let's Encrypt uses it for expiry warnings.
#   - NO port forwarding. If 80 or 443 are forwarded, the three names above
#     resolve to a private address anyway and nothing answers from outside.
#
# Bring it up against the STAGING directory first. Production allows five
# failed validations per hostname per hour, and the mistake that costs them —
# a token without Zone:DNS:Edit on the right zone — is only discoverable by
# trying. Staging has no meaningful limit and issues from an untrusted root, so
# the browser warning is expected and is the signal that everything else
# worked. Set CADDY_ACME_CA in .env, restart Caddy, then clear it back to
# production and restart again.
#
# The default below is the production directory, so an unset variable behaves
# exactly as this file did before it existed.
#
# This file holds only certificate issuance. Everything else — the routes and
# the two snippets they import — is in sites.caddy.
{
	email {$ACME_EMAIL}
	acme_ca {$CADDY_ACME_CA:https://acme-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory}
	# {env.…}, not {$…}: read at runtime rather than substituted when the
	# Caddyfile is parsed, so the token never appears in `caddy adapt` output
	# or in the autosaved JSON config under /caddydata.
	acme_dns cloudflare {env.CF_API_TOKEN}
}

# Two route files, and the split is the point. sites.caddy holds what may be
# public if PUBLIC_HTTP is ever true; admin.caddy holds what must never be, and
# every block in it refuses any client outside RFC1918. Which file a block lives
# in answers "could the internet reach this" without reading the block.
import sites.caddy
import admin.caddy
